WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.101
<c.p>[Begin Tape 4419.

2
00:00:01.101 --> 00:00:02.203
Begin Session I.]

3
00:00:02.203 --> 00:00:02.570
<v DOUGLAS MUNGIN>Perfect.

4
00:00:02.570 --> 00:00:11.380
Today is January twenty-third, 2015 and I’m Douglas Mungin representing the T. Harry Williams Center for Oral History with LSU [Louisiana State University] Libraries.

5
00:00:11.380 --> 00:00:18.722
We are working in conjunction with the Imperial Calcasieu Museum with Bill Shearman to document the history of Mossville, Louisiana.

6
00:00:18.722 --> 00:00:27.165
And to kick that off, we are holding a Mossville oral history day here at the Rigmaiden Recreation Center on Old Spanish Trail.

7
00:00:27.165 --> 00:00:29.000
I’m here today with . . .

8
00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:29.576
<v VERA PAYNE>Vera Payne.

9
00:00:29.576 --> 00:00:31.881
<v MUNGIN>Thank you for agreeing to interview with us.

10
00:00:31.881 --> 00:00:32.458
<v VERA PAYNE>You’re welcome.

11
00:00:32.458 --> 00:00:35.627
<v MUNGIN>This interview hopefully is going to go about fifteen, twenty minutes,

12
00:00:35.627 --> 00:00:40.525
and then we’re planning on setting up a follow up interview with you a little bit later.

13
00:00:40.525 --> 00:00:42.831
So could you please state your full name?

14
00:00:42.831 --> 00:00:43.695
<v PAYNE>Vera J. Payne.

15
00:00:43.695 --> 00:00:46.000
<v MUNGIN>And when and where were you born?

16
00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:48.170
<v PAYNE>I was born in Calcasieu Parish.

17
00:00:48.170 --> 00:00:49.255
<v MUNGIN>And what year?

18
00:00:49.255 --> 00:00:49.617
<v PAYNE>1944.

19
00:00:49.617 --> 00:00:51.787
<v MUNGIN>And do you have any siblings?

20
00:00:51.787 --> 00:00:53.234
<v PAYNE>I have two sisters.

21
00:00:53.234 --> 00:00:55.404
I’m the older of the three.

22
00:00:55.404 --> 00:00:57.213
<v MUNGIN>And what are their names?

23
00:00:57.213 --> 00:00:59.021
<v PAYNE>Margaret Thomas and Deborah Prater.

24
00:00:59.021 --> 00:01:01.191
<v MUNGIN>And what are your parents’ names?

25
00:01:01.191 --> 00:01:03.000
<v PAYNE>Morris and Irene Prater.

26
00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:05.188
<v MUNGIN>And what did they do?

27
00:01:05.188 --> 00:01:09.562
<v PAYNE>Morris Prater is retired, and Irene Prater is a homemaker.

28
00:01:09.562 --> 00:01:17.875
Dad originally worked for . . . In the 50s for Maplewood when they had their processing plant and whatever back there.

29
00:01:17.875 --> 00:01:18.750
<v MUNGIN>What’s Maplewood?

30
00:01:18.750 --> 00:01:23.125
<v PAYNE>Maplewood is a subdivision off to the north of us.

31
00:01:23.125 --> 00:01:23.562
Northwest.

32
00:01:23.562 --> 00:01:25.750
And Mom’s been a homemaker.

33
00:01:25.750 --> 00:01:26.188
<v MUNGIN>Okay.

34
00:01:26.188 --> 00:01:29.250
And your . . . What are your grandparents’ names?

35
00:01:29.250 --> 00:01:34.062
<v PAYNE>My grandparents on my paternal side was Earthy and Eunice Prater.

36
00:01:34.062 --> 00:01:38.438
And on my maternal side was Sophie and Joseph Vincent.

37
00:01:38.438 --> 00:01:43.688
<v MUNGIN>And so did your family . . . so were you born in this area?

38
00:01:43.688 --> 00:01:45.000
In Mossville?

39
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:47.667
<v PAYNE>No, I was not born in this area.

40
00:01:47.667 --> 00:01:50.333
I was born a little north of here.

41
00:01:50.333 --> 00:01:52.000
More toward the DeQuincy area.

42
00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:52.333
<v MUNGIN>Okay.

43
00:01:52.333 --> 00:01:55.000
And when did your family move to Mossville?

44
00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:57.000
<v PAYNE>I was roughly eight years old.

45
00:01:57.000 --> 00:01:57.333
<v MUNGIN>Okay.

46
00:01:57.333 --> 00:01:58.667
What year was that?

47
00:01:58.667 --> 00:01:59.667
<v PAYNE>Probably about 1952.

48
00:01:59.667 --> 00:02:00.667
Something like that.

49
00:02:00.667 --> 00:02:01.000
<v MUNGIN>Okay.

50
00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:05.333
And why did your family move from near the DeQuincy area to Mossville?

51
00:02:05.333 --> 00:02:07.333
<v PAYNE>Because my father’s from this area.

52
00:02:07.333 --> 00:02:09.333
<v MUNGIN>He was originally from this area.

53
00:02:09.333 --> 00:02:09.667
<v PAYNE>Yes.

54
00:02:09.667 --> 00:02:10.000
My . . .

55
00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:11.000
<v MUNGIN>And . . . Sorry.

56
00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:15.865
<v PAYNE>My father’s dad is Earthy Prater that Prater Road was named after.

57
00:02:15.865 --> 00:02:17.892
<v MUNGIN>And who was Earthy Prater?

58
00:02:17.892 --> 00:02:19.108
Your great grandparent . . .

59
00:02:19.108 --> 00:02:20.324
<v PAYNE>My great grandfather.

60
00:02:20.324 --> 00:02:26.811
<v MUNGIN>And so I mean is he one of the original founders of . . . Original families of Mossville?

61
00:02:26.811 --> 00:02:32.486
<v PAYNE>No I think somewhat of a direct descendant, but not one of the original.

62
00:02:32.486 --> 00:02:36.541
<v MUNGIN>So when did your family kind of move into Mossville?

63
00:02:36.541 --> 00:02:41.405
<v PAYNE>My family moved here through the LeDouxs, and the Mosses, and Vincents.

64
00:02:41.405 --> 00:02:45.459
On my other side of the family is the Vincents.

65
00:02:45.459 --> 00:02:52.351
So I guess more or less I’m related on two aspects of the original descendants of Mossville.

66
00:02:52.351 --> 00:02:56.000
<v MUNGIN>And who are the original descendants of Mossville?

67
00:02:56.000 --> 00:03:02.031
<v PAYNE>As far as I know it was the Mosses, Braxtons, Rigmaidens, Vincents, and LeDouxs.

68
00:03:02.031 --> 00:03:02.462
<v MUNGIN>Okay.

69
00:03:02.462 --> 00:03:03.323
<v BILL SHEARMAN>LeDoux family?

70
00:03:03.323 --> 00:03:07.631
<v PAYNE>[Agrees] My . . . According to Dad, my great-grandmother was a LeDoux.

71
00:03:07.631 --> 00:03:16.677
And apparently she owned some property in that two hundred acres that Sasol has negotiated to buy forty some acres from.

72
00:03:16.677 --> 00:03:17.538
<v SHEARMAN>Yes ma’am.

73
00:03:17.538 --> 00:03:22.277
<v PAYNE>I was told she has nine plats of land in there.

74
00:03:22.277 --> 00:03:24.000
<v SHEARMAN>Good for you.

75
00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:24.386
<v PAYNE>Yeah.

76
00:03:24.386 --> 00:03:28.629
<v MUNGIN>So when you moved to Mossville you were eight years old.

77
00:03:28.629 --> 00:03:32.100
What is the earliest memory you have of Mossville?

78
00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:34.414
Like what was that earliest memory?

79
00:03:34.414 --> 00:03:40.586
<v PAYNE>Playing up and down Old Spanish Trail when it was about maybe six and a half,

80
00:03:40.586 --> 00:03:46.757
seven foot wide, only one car could go down on the hard surface at a time.

81
00:03:46.757 --> 00:03:56.400
And you met you met someone from the opposite direction, you had one set of tires on and one set of tires in the gravel.

82
00:03:56.400 --> 00:03:59.486
<v MUNGIN>So how did Mossville look at that time?

83
00:03:59.486 --> 00:04:00.257
<v PAYNE>Very green.

84
00:04:00.257 --> 00:04:00.643
Quiet.

85
00:04:00.643 --> 00:04:01.029
Neighborly.

86
00:04:01.029 --> 00:04:05.657
You could play in other neighbor’s yards and there was no problem.

87
00:04:05.657 --> 00:04:11.057
If you got into trouble in that neighbor’s yard, you got a spanking there,

88
00:04:11.057 --> 00:04:18.000
and when you got home you found out that there was one waiting for you there, also.

89
00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:21.536
<v MUNGIN>So the Prater Road is named after your great . . .

90
00:04:21.536 --> 00:04:21.929
<v PAYNE>Grandfather.

91
00:04:21.929 --> 00:04:22.714
<v MUNGIN>Your grandfather.

92
00:04:22.714 --> 00:04:23.107
<v PAYNE>[Agrees].

93
00:04:23.107 --> 00:04:28.607
<v MUNGIN>Was it . . . Was that road already named after him when you guys moved there?

94
00:04:28.607 --> 00:04:29.000
<v PAYNE>Yes.

95
00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:33.321
That road at one time was nothing but a dirt road.

96
00:04:33.321 --> 00:04:38.429
And every time we would have a hard, hard rain it would flood.

97
00:04:38.429 --> 00:04:48.643
So a group of kids would always get out in the middle of the street with their shoes off and go wading playing in the water.

98
00:04:48.643 --> 00:04:51.786
<v MUNGIN>So who were your neighbors at that time?

99
00:04:51.786 --> 00:04:57.679
<v PAYNE>In my grandmother’s property on Prater Road it was the Hartmans, and let’s see . . . oh,

100
00:04:57.679 --> 00:05:03.964
I’m trying to think of . . . I can see the ladies name there on the corner . . Moutons.

101
00:05:03.964 --> 00:05:09.071
The Moutons, the Hartmans, and then a little farther down was the Gasaways.

102
00:05:09.071 --> 00:05:12.214
And then you got to later the Montgomerys,

103
00:05:12.214 --> 00:05:22.036
and then there was the old Paradise Club that was located on the left just before the railroad track if you going in that direction.

104
00:05:22.036 --> 00:05:24.000
<v MUNGIN>What’s the Paradise Club?

105
00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:26.034
<v PAYNE>That was a night club.

106
00:05:26.034 --> 00:05:31.731
That was the spot when say, the generation before me, we didn’t go there.

107
00:05:31.731 --> 00:05:34.172
I didn’t go there very much.

108
00:05:34.172 --> 00:05:42.310
And I lived oh, basically about a quarter of a mile . . . Where Smith Road is, that was my uncle’s property.

109
00:05:42.310 --> 00:05:43.938
Ava and Leo Prater.

110
00:05:43.938 --> 00:05:49.228
And they had a rent home . . . A rent house on their property also.

111
00:05:49.228 --> 00:05:56.145
And my parents stayed there until they built their home on Moss Avenue, some sixty years ago.

112
00:05:56.145 --> 00:05:56.552
<v MUNGIN>Okay.

113
00:05:56.552 --> 00:05:59.807
And when did your parents build the house?

114
00:05:59.807 --> 00:06:05.910
<v PAYNE>I was roughly about probably ten-ish because it was built before Hurricane Audrey came through.

115
00:06:05.910 --> 00:06:14.455
I remember the morning after Audrey came of through looking through the kitchen window and all I saw was water everywhere.

116
00:06:14.455 --> 00:06:17.710
You could not see the ground any place.

117
00:06:17.710 --> 00:06:18.524
<v MUNGIN>How did . . .?

118
00:06:18.524 --> 00:06:20.152
<v SHEARMAN>Did the home flood?

119
00:06:20.152 --> 00:06:23.000
<v PAYNE>No, the home did not flood.

120
00:06:23.000 --> 00:06:26.716
<v MUNGIN>How did it affect Mossville and like the surrounding areas?

121
00:06:26.716 --> 00:06:27.088
<v PAYNE>The . . .

122
00:06:27.088 --> 00:06:27.831
<v MUNGIN>Hurricane Audrey.

123
00:06:27.831 --> 00:06:28.574
<v PAYNE>The hurricane?

124
00:06:28.574 --> 00:06:36.378
Well most homes at that time were built on piers so I don’t think there was hardly any flooding or anything.

125
00:06:36.378 --> 00:06:41.581
Some of the homes that wasn’t constructed very well, they had damage to them.

126
00:06:41.581 --> 00:06:46.041
And people had to try to repair them the best they could.

127
00:06:46.041 --> 00:06:49.757
There wasn’t a lot of financial resources in that day.

128
00:06:49.757 --> 00:06:52.358
<v MUNGIN>So what did your house look like?

129
00:06:52.358 --> 00:06:54.216
Could you describe your property?

130
00:06:54.216 --> 00:06:55.331
Your family’s property?

131
00:06:55.331 --> 00:06:57.561
<v PAYNE>It was three bedrooms, two bedrooms.

132
00:06:57.561 --> 00:07:06.108
My parents had the front bedroom, I had the back bedroom, one bathroom off from a hall, a living room, and a kitchen.

133
00:07:06.108 --> 00:07:13.912
So I guess the total space at that particular time was maybe fifty, fifty-five foot long, and about forty foot wide.

134
00:07:13.912 --> 00:07:16.885
<v MUNGIN>And did you guys have like a garden?

135
00:07:16.885 --> 00:07:18.000
Any yard?

136
00:07:18.000 --> 00:07:22.585
<v PAYNE>Mom planted flowers and at that time Dad wasn’t really a gardener.

137
00:07:22.585 --> 00:07:26.789
He didn’t become a gardener until he got probably past fifty.

138
00:07:26.789 --> 00:07:32.138
And then he started planting turnips, and string beans, and different things like that.

139
00:07:32.138 --> 00:07:36.341
And he planted those until last summer when he moved away.

140
00:07:36.341 --> 00:07:38.634
<v MUNGIN>Was your mom a good gardener?

141
00:07:38.634 --> 00:07:40.163
<v PAYNE>Mom didn’t like gardening.

142
00:07:40.163 --> 00:07:42.455
Mom took care of the house.

143
00:07:42.455 --> 00:07:46.276
She was a fantastic cook and she kept clean house.

144
00:07:46.276 --> 00:07:47.805
<v MUNGIN>What did she cook?

145
00:07:47.805 --> 00:07:54.301
<v PAYNE>She makes one of the best banana puddings from scratch that you could wrap your tongue around.

146
00:07:54.301 --> 00:07:55.447
<v MUNGIN>With vanilla wafers?

147
00:07:55.447 --> 00:07:59.650
<v PAYNE>With the vanilla wafers, and the homemade custard pudding, and whatever.

148
00:07:59.650 --> 00:08:02.325
And she’s a fantastic potato pie baker.

149
00:08:02.325 --> 00:08:05.000
Those are two of her specialties.

150
00:08:05.000 --> 00:08:07.000
<v MUNGIN>Did she pass that down to you?

151
00:08:07.000 --> 00:08:09.000
<v PAYNE>She tried, but without any success [laughs].

152
00:08:09.000 --> 00:08:15.857
My first pudding after she gave me the recipe, you would take a spoon and it would just drip off from the spoon [laughs].

153
00:08:15.857 --> 00:08:18.429
<v MUNGIN>That’s exactly what happened with me and my mom.

154
00:08:18.429 --> 00:08:18.714
<v PAYNE>Yeah.

155
00:08:18.714 --> 00:08:22.429
<v MUNGIN>Like she’d try to tell me and I was like . . . It became soup.

156
00:08:22.429 --> 00:08:23.000
<v PAYNE>I know.

157
00:08:23.000 --> 00:08:23.571
I know.

158
00:08:23.571 --> 00:08:24.714
Wasn’t good at all.

159
00:08:24.714 --> 00:08:27.000
<v MUNGIN>What church did your family go to?

160
00:08:27.000 --> 00:08:33.857
<v PAYNE>My father’s family went to Mount Zion, which is the oldest church in this area.

161
00:08:33.857 --> 00:08:41.629
And my family went to a church that was a branch of Mount Zion, which is Willow

162
00:08:41.629 --> 00:08:42.086
<c.p>Springs.

163
00:08:42.086 --> 00:08:49.857
Mount Zion is a hundred forty plus years, and Willow Spring is a hundred five years old.

164
00:08:49.857 --> 00:08:54.429
<v MUNGIN>Do you know why the church . . . Why they split off?

165
00:08:54.429 --> 00:09:04.029
<v PAYNE>Because my Vincent side of the family lived in the Willow Spring area and that was . . . Dad married into the family.

166
00:09:04.029 --> 00:09:07.229
So eventually Dad migrated to Mom’s church.

167
00:09:07.229 --> 00:09:07.686
<v MUNGIN>Okay.

168
00:09:07.686 --> 00:09:12.257
And who were some of the leaders in the church?

169
00:09:12.257 --> 00:09:17.286
<v PAYNE>Dallas Moss . . . Oh, God why can’t I think of his name?

170
00:09:17.286 --> 00:09:18.200
Jim Johnson.

171
00:09:18.200 --> 00:09:21.400
And they were some . . . And Ervin Perkins.

172
00:09:21.400 --> 00:09:24.600
They were some of the early leaders.

173
00:09:24.600 --> 00:09:30.086
And at Willow Springs there was a Walter Smith, and Joe Vincent.

174
00:09:30.086 --> 00:09:38.771
Walter Smith and James Moss I think were kind of wealthy at that particular day and time in standing.

175
00:09:38.771 --> 00:09:47.000
Walter Smith had a sugarcane mill, and Jim Moss apparently had a mill or something that he ran,

176
00:09:47.000 --> 00:09:53.400
and then later the post office that he kind of sublet to someone else.

177
00:09:53.400 --> 00:10:00.257
We didn’t know that term at the time, but that was what was going on.

178
00:10:00.257 --> 00:10:03.000
<v MUNGIN>What happened to the mills?

179
00:10:03.000 --> 00:10:06.496
<v PAYNE>Eventually I guess it just closed down with modernization.

180
00:10:06.496 --> 00:10:07.272
Times changed.

181
00:10:07.272 --> 00:10:10.379
<v MUNGIN>Like what year . . . Like what timeframe was that?

182
00:10:10.379 --> 00:10:17.759
<v PAYNE>The sugarcane mill that Walter Smith operated phased out probably when I was about four or five years old.

183
00:10:17.759 --> 00:10:20.478
And the reason I say that was,

184
00:10:20.478 --> 00:10:33.683
he kind of became like my pal because I was the oldest grandchild on that side of the family and sometimes he would take me to go to what was called his “branding pen”.

185
00:10:33.683 --> 00:10:37.179
And brand cows, and feed his horses, and whatever.

186
00:10:37.179 --> 00:10:40.286
And I remember walking along his old mill.

187
00:10:40.286 --> 00:10:47.665
And then on this end of the retrospect, I can more or less remember just church services and whatever.

188
00:10:47.665 --> 00:10:56.598
But there was an old, old resident called Pete Moss at the time that lived in what we would call today a shanty.

189
00:10:56.598 --> 00:11:00.094
And I guess he was very happy and content.

190
00:11:00.094 --> 00:11:02.424
He had a lot of dogs.

191
00:11:02.424 --> 00:11:04.366
His wife name was Virginia.

192
00:11:04.366 --> 00:11:07.862
And he would travel back and forth by wagon.

193
00:11:07.862 --> 00:11:12.134
And I was just a little girl at that particular time.

194
00:11:12.134 --> 00:11:14.853
Probably twelve years old at the oldest.

195
00:11:14.853 --> 00:11:17.183
Twelve, thirteen at the most, see.

196
00:11:17.183 --> 00:11:22.621
And it just so happened that the property that my home is sitting on,

197
00:11:22.621 --> 00:11:28.446
I purchased that from, I think his brother, or one at least of his ancestors.

198
00:11:28.446 --> 00:11:29.223
<v MUNGIN>Oh, wow.

199
00:11:29.223 --> 00:11:30.000
<v PAYNE>Yeah.

200
00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:32.723
<v MUNGIN>Where did you go to school?

201
00:11:32.723 --> 00:11:36.807
<v PAYNE>I went to school at two basically different places.

202
00:11:36.807 --> 00:11:41.798
There was an elementary school in north Sulphur called Willow Springs.

203
00:11:41.798 --> 00:11:47.697
It was a two room school that had very high windows over it.

204
00:11:47.697 --> 00:11:52.689
And it taught grades like probably one through four something another.

205
00:11:52.689 --> 00:12:00.403
And then after we moved to Mossville, on probably close to this site was two structured buildings.

206
00:12:00.403 --> 00:12:08.571
One had the elementary probably through grades four, and then probably five and six was in the other

207
00:12:08.571 --> 00:12:09.025
<c.p>building.

208
00:12:09.025 --> 00:12:14.017
And Mrs. Verly Mae Washington was the principal at the time.

209
00:12:14.017 --> 00:12:24.000
And then when Mossville opened in ’55 we all transferred to that, and Ms. Washington became assistant principal to Principal Armstrong.

210
00:12:24.000 --> 00:12:26.963
<v MUNGIN>And Principal Armstrong, was he a community leader?

211
00:12:26.963 --> 00:12:28.074
Being the principal?

212
00:12:28.074 --> 00:12:29.185
<v PAYNE>Yes he was.

213
00:12:29.185 --> 00:12:34.741
He also lived in this community, not too far from where my home is now.

214
00:12:34.741 --> 00:12:40.296
They had purchased a home, he and his wife Ethel, and they had one son.

215
00:12:40.296 --> 00:12:47.704
And they lived there, so therefore he became a part of the community and not just the principal, you see.

216
00:12:47.704 --> 00:12:48.074
<v MUNGIN>Okay.

217
00:12:48.074 --> 00:12:51.037
Who were your favorite teachers at that school?

218
00:12:51.037 --> 00:12:52.519
<v PAYNE>Ava Prater, my aunt.

219
00:12:52.519 --> 00:12:54.741
She was a home ec teacher.

220
00:12:54.741 --> 00:13:00.667
Didn’t like being in a class at the time at all because she was a stickler.

221
00:13:00.667 --> 00:13:06.222
If it was a five eight seam, it had to be a five eight seam.

222
00:13:06.222 --> 00:13:11.037
And Gloria Rogers, who was Gloria Kilgore at the time, she’s still alive.

223
00:13:11.037 --> 00:13:18.074
And if I can quote her right, “The moving finger writeth upon the wall, and having written, moveth on.”

224
00:13:18.074 --> 00:13:19.185
<v MUNGIN>And moves on.

225
00:13:19.185 --> 00:13:24.000
<v SHEARMAN>Nor all your piety, nor your tears, can erase a line of . . .

226
00:13:24.000 --> 00:13:24.357
<v PAYNE>Right.

227
00:13:24.357 --> 00:13:26.500
<v SHEARMAN>Do you know where it’s from?

228
00:13:26.500 --> 00:13:26.857
<v PAYNE>[Agrees].

229
00:13:26.857 --> 00:13:27.571
Omar Khayyam.

230
00:13:27.571 --> 00:13:28.286
<v SHEARMAN>The Rubaiyat.

231
00:13:28.286 --> 00:13:29.000
<v PAYNE>The Rubaiyat.

232
00:13:29.000 --> 00:13:29.357
Yeah.

233
00:13:29.357 --> 00:13:37.214
So what she was telling you is, if you didn’t get it together it was going to move on without you [laughs].

234
00:13:37.214 --> 00:13:39.714
And she’s still alive today going strong.

235
00:13:39.714 --> 00:13:40.071
Yeah.

236
00:13:40.071 --> 00:13:42.214
<v MUNGIN>And what year did you graduate?

237
00:13:42.214 --> 00:13:44.000
<v PAYNE>I graduated in 1962.

238
00:13:44.000 --> 00:13:47.886
<v MUNGIN>And did you decide to go to college?

239
00:13:47.886 --> 00:13:58.571
<v PAYNE>I went to college, and this is quite embarrassing, at the University of Lafayette with one of my classmates, Curtis Jean Pipkins.

240
00:13:58.571 --> 00:14:05.371
And at that time I was so in love that my boyfriend, Wilfred Payne,

241
00:14:05.371 --> 00:14:13.143
and Ralph January would come to Lafayette to visit us the weekends that we didn’t come.

242
00:14:13.143 --> 00:14:17.514
Needless to say, I flunked out big time [laughs].

243
00:14:17.514 --> 00:14:19.943
I flunked out big time.

244
00:14:19.943 --> 00:14:31.114
So after I married Wilfred and saw the need for an education, that’s when I went back to school and became a nurse.

245
00:14:31.114 --> 00:14:32.086
<v MUNGIN>Oh, nice.

246
00:14:32.086 --> 00:14:35.000
When did you become a nurse?

247
00:14:35.000 --> 00:14:38.886
<v PAYNE>I became an L . . . it was in California.

248
00:14:38.886 --> 00:14:44.229
An LVN in 1972 I believe it was, ’71 or ’72.

249
00:14:44.229 --> 00:14:48.600
And we stayed there eight years and then as . . .

250
00:14:48.600 --> 00:14:50.543
<v MUNGIN>Which part of California?

251
00:14:50.543 --> 00:14:52.000
<v PAYNE>Los Angeles.

252
00:14:52.000 --> 00:14:52.365
<v MUNGIN>Okay.

253
00:14:52.365 --> 00:14:53.461
<v PAYNE>West Los Angles.

254
00:14:53.461 --> 00:15:00.765
And I worked at some of the biggest hospitals there: Cedars Sinai, Hollywood Presbyterian, Kaiser Permanente . . . Did a little psych . . .

255
00:15:00.765 --> 00:15:02.957
<v MUNGIN>I was born in Hollywood Presbyterian.

256
00:15:02.957 --> 00:15:04.417
<v PAYNE>You were born in . . .?

257
00:15:04.417 --> 00:15:04.783
Yes.

258
00:15:04.783 --> 00:15:06.974
And then we moved back here.

259
00:15:06.974 --> 00:15:19.026
And the reason we moved back here was we looked at homes in California and all of them were forty and fifty years old and they were asking a small fortune for it.

260
00:15:19.026 --> 00:15:24.139
And we decided that we could move back here and build a home cheaper.

261
00:15:24.139 --> 00:15:32.539
So we built a home that was basically 2700 square foot under the roof and it only cost us about forty-five thousand dollars.

262
00:15:32.539 --> 00:15:34.000
<v SHEARMAN>Here in Mossville?

263
00:15:34.000 --> 00:15:34.571
<v PAYNE>In Mossville.

264
00:15:34.571 --> 00:15:36.000
<v SHEARMAN>What year was that?

265
00:15:36.000 --> 00:15:36.822
<v PAYNE>In ’78.

266
00:15:36.822 --> 00:15:44.216
We paid a contractor by the name of Fontenot to lay the foundation and . . . you hear my tummy?

267
00:15:44.216 --> 00:15:46.681
I hadn’t ate . . . Had lunch today.

268
00:15:46.681 --> 00:15:49.146
And do the blocking us in.

269
00:15:49.146 --> 00:15:56.951
My husband and his father Wilson Payne and a man by the name of Sims did all the brickwork.

270
00:15:56.951 --> 00:16:05.989
A teenager that was in high school by the name of Paul Gradney, and we call him Bucky, but he’s a Prater.

271
00:16:05.989 --> 00:16:10.097
Dad’s nephew and I, we put up all the sheetrock.

272
00:16:10.097 --> 00:16:11.330
All the paneling.

273
00:16:11.330 --> 00:16:19.135
And Dad and an uncle of mine that’s deceased, [Elfie?] Dixon, they cut all the molding and whatever else.

274
00:16:19.135 --> 00:16:23.243
We only hired a contractor for . . . To frame us up.

275
00:16:23.243 --> 00:16:31.870
And a friend gave us a discount doing the plumbing and he lived right across the street on . . . Two streets over.

276
00:16:31.870 --> 00:16:33.103
A Mr. Charles.

277
00:16:33.103 --> 00:16:36.800
And Leon Murray, who’s deceased, did our electrical work.

278
00:16:36.800 --> 00:16:44.195
My goal is to try to redo the same thing at not too much of an increasing budget.

279
00:16:44.195 --> 00:16:46.249
<v SHEARMAN>You sold that home there?

280
00:16:46.249 --> 00:16:47.481
<v PAYNE>Not yet, no.

281
00:16:47.481 --> 00:16:49.535
<v SHEARMAN>Are you still in it?

282
00:16:49.535 --> 00:16:51.178
<v PAYNE>I’m still in it.

283
00:16:51.178 --> 00:16:52.000
<v SHEARMAN>Okay.

284
00:16:52.000 --> 00:16:52.458
<v PAYNE>Yes.

285
00:16:52.458 --> 00:17:01.153
I also have in that . . . In my home, a pew from Mount Zion Church that’s a hundred years old.

286
00:17:01.153 --> 00:17:03.441
<v SHEARMAN>How did you steal that?

287
00:17:03.441 --> 00:17:07.102
<v PAYNE>A lot of people don’t like old things.

288
00:17:07.102 --> 00:17:07.559
<v SHEARMAN>Really?

289
00:17:07.559 --> 00:17:10.305
<v PAYNE>So they get rid of them.

290
00:17:10.305 --> 00:17:11.678
<v SHEARMAN>Is it cypress [?]?

291
00:17:11.678 --> 00:17:13.051
<v PAYNE>It probably is.

292
00:17:13.051 --> 00:17:14.881
I love old things.

293
00:17:14.881 --> 00:17:18.085
I have my great-grandparents’ old RCA crank . . .

294
00:17:18.085 --> 00:17:19.000
<v SHEARMAN>[Seventy-eight?]

295
00:17:19.000 --> 00:17:19.338
<v PAYNE>Yes.

296
00:17:19.338 --> 00:17:19.676
Yes.

297
00:17:19.676 --> 00:17:20.689
<v SHEARMAN>Good for you.

298
00:17:20.689 --> 00:17:23.392
<v PAYNE>Doesn’t work, but the frame looks good [laughs].

299
00:17:23.392 --> 00:17:24.405
<v SHEARMAN>Nobody knows better.

300
00:17:24.405 --> 00:17:27.446
<v PAYNE>And I use it for a piece of furniture.

301
00:17:27.446 --> 00:17:27.784
Yeah.

302
00:17:27.784 --> 00:17:28.122
Definitely.

303
00:17:28.122 --> 00:17:36.568
And then back to my schooling, so I was roughly forty-two years old and I went back to college in Texas and became an RN.

304
00:17:36.568 --> 00:17:36.905
<v MUNGIN>Cool.

305
00:17:36.905 --> 00:17:40.284
What were some of the most important places in Mossville?

306
00:17:40.284 --> 00:17:44.000
Like while you were growing up, and like even now?

307
00:17:44.000 --> 00:17:45.188
<v PAYNE>The swimming pool.

308
00:17:45.188 --> 00:17:49.146
When it was summer we could all hang out there.

309
00:17:49.146 --> 00:17:55.083
Once we got our chores done, we were free for the rest of the day.

310
00:17:55.083 --> 00:18:03.000
There was a little place farther down just before you get in the bend of the curve called Ms. Martill’s.

311
00:18:03.000 --> 00:18:04.583
She was a Braxton.

312
00:18:04.583 --> 00:18:04.979
Okay.

313
00:18:04.979 --> 00:18:07.750
And it was like a little canteen.

314
00:18:07.750 --> 00:18:09.729
Kids could hang out there.

315
00:18:09.729 --> 00:18:17.250
Also right across the street, later Ms. Lula Lyons had a little canteen and we could hang out there.

316
00:18:17.250 --> 00:18:19.625
So those were some fun spots.

317
00:18:19.625 --> 00:18:22.000
<v MUNGIN>Where was the grocery store?

318
00:18:22.000 --> 00:18:22.389
<v PAYNE>Ha!

319
00:18:22.389 --> 00:18:30.161
Well, later probably mid-50s on Prater Road there was a white family that moved there by the name of Rougeau.

320
00:18:30.161 --> 00:18:32.104
They had a grocery store.

321
00:18:32.104 --> 00:18:39.488
And before that there was a little store right on the other side of the railroad track farther down.

322
00:18:39.488 --> 00:18:41.043
He was a Garrett.

323
00:18:41.043 --> 00:18:41.820
Ira Garrett.

324
00:18:41.820 --> 00:18:43.763
He had a little store.

325
00:18:43.763 --> 00:18:48.815
Then sometimes later, the same man Smith that I was telling you about.

326
00:18:48.815 --> 00:18:49.592
Walter Smith.

327
00:18:49.592 --> 00:18:55.810
He had a son that had a service station just a little bit past Prater Road.

328
00:18:55.810 --> 00:19:03.583
And they would have like chips and different things like that that you could get, but, in order to make

329
00:19:03.583 --> 00:19:07.858
<c.p>groceries when I was a child, every Saturday rain or shine,

330
00:19:07.858 --> 00:19:14.076
my grandfather Earthy Prater would saddle up his horse, or hitch his horse to a wagon,

331
00:19:14.076 --> 00:19:21.071
and he would travel from Prater Road to Westlake and I’m trying to think of . . . was it Managan?

332
00:19:21.071 --> 00:19:22.626
Krauss &amp; Managan or something?

333
00:19:22.626 --> 00:19:23.403
<v SHEARMAN>Yes ma’am.

334
00:19:23.403 --> 00:19:23.791
<v PAYNE>Okay.

335
00:19:23.791 --> 00:19:28.455
The grocery store over there, and that’s where he would purchase groceries.

336
00:19:28.455 --> 00:19:31.175
By that time, Dad had a car.

337
00:19:31.175 --> 00:19:37.782
So there was a little plaza that was in Maplewood, and there was an A&amp;P grocery store.

338
00:19:37.782 --> 00:19:40.114
Now, this was in the 50s.

339
00:19:40.114 --> 00:19:44.000
And Mom and then would make groceries from there.

340
00:19:44.000 --> 00:19:47.802
<v MUNGIN>So we’ve heard some things about voter registration in this area.

341
00:19:47.802 --> 00:19:50.222
Do you have any memories of that?

342
00:19:50.222 --> 00:19:58.173
<v PAYNE>No. I don’t because if there was a problem with voter registration, it would have probably come the later 50s and early 60s.

343
00:19:58.173 --> 00:20:02.667
And at that time, I didn’t really have too much of a problem.

344
00:20:02.667 --> 00:20:06.815
I registered to vote as soon as I could register to vote.

345
00:20:06.815 --> 00:20:13.037
And I was working at Saint Pat’s as a nurse’s aide, so I got around quite a bit.

346
00:20:13.037 --> 00:20:14.420
I didn’t experience that.

347
00:20:14.420 --> 00:20:15.802
Maybe some people did.

348
00:20:15.802 --> 00:20:24.099
And then when a lot of the other took place with the marches and everything I had already moved to California by that time.

349
00:20:24.099 --> 00:20:26.864
So I missed out on some of that.

350
00:20:26.864 --> 00:20:30.667
There may have been some, but I’m not aware of it.

351
00:20:30.667 --> 00:20:31.012
<v MUNGIN>Okay.

352
00:20:31.012 --> 00:20:38.272
So how has the community changed from when you grew up, and then you ended up leaving, and then coming back?

353
00:20:38.272 --> 00:20:40.000
Like during those transitions?

354
00:20:40.000 --> 00:20:44.218
<v PAYNE>When I came back, there wasn’t that much of a change.

355
00:20:44.218 --> 00:20:48.820
It was still the friendliness, it was still the neighborness, and whatever.

356
00:20:48.820 --> 00:20:55.338
But as others began to come into the area, and I guess families began to grow up,

357
00:20:55.338 --> 00:21:00.323
and I can only say that in reference to basically my own family.

358
00:21:00.323 --> 00:21:03.391
Like Mom had three sisters and one brother.

359
00:21:03.391 --> 00:21:11.827
Well, their brother died at age fifty and Mom and two of her sisters live within a two and half block radius.

360
00:21:11.827 --> 00:21:18.729
And my other sister still stayed in the Willow Spring . . . Aunt still stayed in the Willow Spring area.

361
00:21:18.729 --> 00:21:22.564
Well when we were growing up, there was always picnics.

362
00:21:22.564 --> 00:21:29.850
Like at Fourth of July, or we’ll get together for Christmas, or we’ll do this, or we’ll do that.

363
00:21:29.850 --> 00:21:32.534
And the family was really close knit.

364
00:21:32.534 --> 00:21:40.586
But I think as the children began to grow up, starting with my generation, and the need to do other things,

365
00:21:40.586 --> 00:21:46.722
get involved in certain civic activities, your job, and whatever, it pulls the family closer apart.

366
00:21:46.722 --> 00:21:49.406
Today, for instance, with my cousin passing.

367
00:21:49.406 --> 00:21:55.925
Now, I hadn’t talked to my cousin probably in two to three weeks prior to her hospitalization.

368
00:21:55.925 --> 00:22:02.827
But we’re the type of family that we know when there’s a need, we’re there for each other.

369
00:22:02.827 --> 00:22:06.662
And I think that happened to the community at large.

370
00:22:06.662 --> 00:22:14.714
Everybody just kind of pulled away from each other and kind of started doing their own little thing and whatever else.

371
00:22:14.714 --> 00:22:19.699
And then some of the people moved away to different areas and whatever.

372
00:22:19.699 --> 00:22:22.000
So we lost that cohesiveness.

373
00:22:22.000 --> 00:22:23.556
<v MUNGIN>Last three questions.

374
00:22:23.556 --> 00:22:26.667
What does home mean to you?

375
00:22:26.667 --> 00:22:29.778
<v PAYNE>Home is a place of security.

376
00:22:29.778 --> 00:22:40.148
A place where one should be able to be themselves whether it’s good, bad, or indifferent and still be accepted.

377
00:22:40.148 --> 00:22:47.926
A home is a place where someone knows without a doubt that they’re loved unconditionally.

378
00:22:47.926 --> 00:22:50.000
It’s a safe haven.

379
00:22:50.000 --> 00:22:52.593
<v MUNGIN>Is Mossville home for you?

380
00:22:52.593 --> 00:22:58.815
<v PAYNE>It was that way until probably the last six to twelve months.

381
00:22:58.815 --> 00:22:59.333
Probably.

382
00:22:59.333 --> 00:23:00.370
I’d say.

383
00:23:00.370 --> 00:23:04.000
<v MUNGIN>What does community mean to you?

384
00:23:04.000 --> 00:23:14.248
<v PAYNE>A place where a group of people may not all have the same interests, but there’s an overall goal that they work for.

385
00:23:14.248 --> 00:23:22.713
Whether it’s everybody keeping their yard up, or most people belonging to some type of church or something another.

386
00:23:22.713 --> 00:23:25.832
You have some of the same values.

387
00:23:25.832 --> 00:23:28.505
<v MUNGIN>Is that still present today here?

388
00:23:28.505 --> 00:23:28.950
<v PAYNE>No.

389
00:23:28.950 --> 00:23:31.178
<v MUNGIN>When did that go away?

390
00:23:31.178 --> 00:23:36.970
<v PAYNE>And it didn’t totally have to do with I don’t think the plants.

391
00:23:36.970 --> 00:23:41.426
I think it was just something that evolved over time.

392
00:23:41.426 --> 00:23:41.871
Yeah.

393
00:23:41.871 --> 00:23:49.000
<v SHEARMAN>Do you think the population wouldn’t have been diminished had . . . even with the plants here?

394
00:23:49.000 --> 00:23:50.828
<v PAYNE>I am not sure.

395
00:23:50.828 --> 00:23:54.483
That would be a hard question to answer.

396
00:23:54.483 --> 00:23:55.397
It would.

397
00:23:55.397 --> 00:24:05.450
Some people moved away after they graduated from high school to go to different areas, so that was a loss within itself.

398
00:24:05.450 --> 00:24:13.675
Some did come back, some remain here, and I can’t really knock the plants because without the plants,

399
00:24:13.675 --> 00:24:20.530
the economy would not have grown as fast as it did in this particular area.

400
00:24:20.530 --> 00:24:24.642
So you can’t bite the hand that feeds you.

401
00:24:24.642 --> 00:24:25.556
<v SHEARMAN>Yes ma’am.

402
00:24:25.556 --> 00:24:26.013
<v PAYNE>Yeah.

403
00:24:26.013 --> 00:24:27.384
That’s my philosophy.

404
00:24:27.384 --> 00:24:27.841
<v MUNGIN>Alright.

405
00:24:27.841 --> 00:24:28.755
Last question.

406
00:24:28.755 --> 00:24:34.238
What’s the most important thing you want people to remember about Mossville?

407
00:24:34.238 --> 00:24:36.066
<v PAYNE>That we were here.

408
00:24:36.066 --> 00:24:42.007
Somewhere somehow through the next hundred or plus years if this world continues,

409
00:24:42.007 --> 00:24:49.318
to remember that there was a community, Mossville, and that it was settled right after slavery.

410
00:24:49.318 --> 00:24:55.715
And that group of people took nothing and did make something out of it.

411
00:24:55.715 --> 00:24:56.629
<v MUNGIN>Thank you.

412
00:24:56.629 --> 00:24:58.000
<v PAYNE>You’re welcome.

413
00:24:58.000 --> 00:25:02.512
<v MUNGIN>Is there any last thing you would like to say?

414
00:25:02.512 --> 00:25:13.793
<v PAYNE>I’m just very glad that this history is being processed because that way maybe my grandchildren didn’t get something that I tried to teach them,

415
00:25:13.793 --> 00:25:21.012
or something that I missed, that they can go back one day thumb through the pages,

416
00:25:21.012 --> 00:25:26.878
or look on the screen, and say, “I didn’t know this took place.”

417
00:25:26.878 --> 00:25:30.037
And it will mean that we mattered.

418
00:25:30.037 --> 00:25:30.939
<v MUNGIN>Thank you.

419
00:25:30.939 --> 00:25:31.841
<v PAYNE>You’re welcome.

420
00:25:31.841 --> 00:25:32.293
<v MUNGIN>Alright.

421
00:25:32.293 --> 00:25:35.000
So that concludes our interview.

422
00:25:35.000 --> 00:25:35.200
<c.p>[End Tape 4419.

423
00:25:35.000 --> 00:25:35.200
End Session I.]
